intro:
A video about CPU caches shows this function, and almost nothing in it looks like the C++ of a first course:

```cpp norun
auto MeasureL1CacheLatency() -> void {
    constexpr auto CacheLineSize = std::hardware_destructive_interference_size;
    constexpr auto EvictCacheLineSize = CacheLineSize * (2 << 12);
    constexpr auto Trials = 20'000;
    std::vector<uint8_t> CacheLineData(CacheLineSize), CacheLineEvictData(EvictCacheLineSize);
    volatile uint64_t sink = 0;
    auto floodTime = 0.0;
    for (auto trials{0uz}; trials < Trials.size(); trials++) {
        auto floodStart = steady_clock::now();
        auto floodEnd = steady_clock::now();
    }
}
```

In the video's editor, gray text like `->void`, `:size_t`, and `:unsigned long` sat after some names, and `.size()` had a red underline. So where does all of it come from? Is it OS-specific? No. Every line is standard C++, from C++98 to C++23, and the one thing that depends on the machine is a number the compiler picks. The red underline is real: `Trials` is an `int`, which has no `.size()`, so that line doesn't compile anywhere.

This lesson sorts code by where it comes from, in four layers: the language and its library, the choices the standard leaves to each implementation, the operating system and the hardware under the library, and the extensions only some compilers have. Each part shows the sandbox (x86-64 Linux, g++ 10.2, libstdc++) beside this Mac (ARM64, Apple clang 21, libc++), and how to look a thing up yourself.

